import{_ as o,o as t,c as n,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"1.1 So Tell Me, What Is Blocking Control?","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"1.1 那我问你，什么是阻塞控制","archivedAt":"2026-11-14","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS.md"}'),a={name:"1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-production-controlled-by-blocking.md"};function s(l,e,r,c,p,d){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="_1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-blocking-control" tabindex="-1">1.1 So Tell Me, What Is Blocking Control? <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-1-so-tell-me-what-is-blocking-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1\\.1 So Tell Me, What Is Blocking Control?&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=quUTb9JSv879txqKEKI0BS" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>In automatic industrial production in Endfield, intelligent components—such as a valve opening when inventory reaches a threshold—are lacking. At present, perhaps the only widely used signals are <span class="mark-red"><strong>clock signals and blocking signals</strong></span>. This tutorial mainly discusses <span class="mark-red"><strong>blocking signals</strong></span> in automatic production.</p><blockquote><p>Clock signals can also produce remarkable lines. For example, I used them for single-Forge Heavy Xiranite production <a href="/endfield-skydoc/single-forge-heavy-xiranite-using-clock-signals">Single-Forge Heavy Xiranite Using Clock Signals</a>. The signal-control discussion in the lower half of this page also covers this. Other members are welcome to cite related results here.</p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/producing-xircon-effluent-in-a-single-reactor-crucible">Producing Xircon Effluent in a Single Reactor Crucible</a></p></blockquote><p>Just as changing from flowing to blocked in a conveyor–Splitter–Converger system causes flow to be <u>redistributed</u> (see <a href="/endfield-skydoc/flow-theory-for-idealized-logistics-systems">Flow Theory for Idealized Systems</a>), <strong>blocking within a production process also redistributes flow and thus changes production</strong>. We can use this property to achieve <strong>automatic switching between production plans</strong> and meet various advanced production goals.</p><blockquote><p>Automatic switching means that, without manual player intervention such as rearranging or changing facility states, a line changes its production scheme using internally generated signals such as blocking or periodicity.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Such production is, to an extent, equivalent to mathematically solving flows on a blocking graph. Compared with blocking-flow theory, however, it adds machines that transform item streams, connecting more closely to practical applications while introducing many new problems.</p></blockquote><p>Several practically meaningful signals can be reduced to blocking signals in particular circumstances:</p><ol><li><p><span class="mark-purple">A machine produces at full or a specified rate → its input buffer blocks, except at the critical case where supply rate equals production rate.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">Product storage is full → the producing machine&#39;s output buffer blocks, and its input buffer blocks as a consequence.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">Flow reaches 6/m → a control port limited to 6 creates blocking.</span></p></li><li><p><span class="mark-purple">In fluid-gas-transmuter feedback, Liquid Xiranite is supplied only when the input fluid is sufficient → the input fluid blocks the Liquid Xiranite recirculation loop, routing it into the transmuter&#39;s supply path.</span></p></li></ol><p>We can therefore deliberately build structures that may become blocked, according to our actual production needs, and use the flow changes under different blocking states to achieve automatic production. The principles of blocking control examine <strong>how to build these structures, under what conditions they become blocked, how that blocking redistributes flow in the line, and whether the final production goal can be achieved</strong>.</p><p>Blocking control is not an arcane theory. It only asks you to think one step further from the production lines you already have. You may already have seen or used it in many places; the more important point is how to recognize the phenomenon.</p><blockquote><p>Reaching storage capacity is one form of blocking, so how lines change when storage fills is an important topic too (full-storage-dynamics.jpg).</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>If basic line design means making products at rate y* from a given quantity or input rate x*—the finished report looks correct—f(x*) = y*, this article discusses the next level: how the line responds to external changes such as input supply or full product storage, and how to use those responses for more complex production needs. Does f give the correct y for every x in some range? When supply x* changes, does y* reflect the desired result? Or when y* changes, can x* respond appropriately?</p></blockquote><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/1-2-what-are-the-benefits-of-blocking-based-production">1.2 What Are the Benefits of Blocking Control?</a></p></div>',1)])])}const g=o(a,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
